"cronokirby - (2026-02) Composition Theorems for Zero-Knowledge IOPs (https://cronokirby.com/refs/2026-02-composition-theorems-for-zero-knowledge-iops.html)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cronokirby.com/refs/2026-02-composition-theorems-for-zero-knowledge-iops.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 19\nL0: 1970-01-01\nL1: \nL2: # 【0†(2026-02) Composition Theorems for Zero-Knowledge IOPs†eprint.iacr.org】\nL3: \nL4:   * Himanshu Vashishth\nL5:   * Mor Weiss\nL6: \nL7: 2026-02-18\nL8: \nL9:   * 【1†#cryptography】\nL10:   * 【2†#paper】\nL11: # Abstract\nL12: > Interactive Oracle Proofs (IOPs) enable a probabilistic verifier interacting with a prover to verify NP statements while reading only few bits from the prover messages. Zero-Knowledge IOPs (ZK-IOPs) have the additional guarantee that a query-bounded (possibly malicious) verifier learns nothing about the NP witness.\nL13: >\nL14: > We initiate a systematic study of ZK preservation under IOP composition, and prove general composition theorems for ZK-IOPs in the 2- and multi-IOP setting. Our main result shows that ZK is preserved in the setting of perfect, black-box, straight-line ZK (the standard setting for ZK-IOPs), if the outer IOP has an additional mild property that is satisfied by existing ZK-IOPs.\nL15: Contrary to common belief, this does not follow from composition theorems for multiparty protocols (Kushilevitz, Lindell and Rabin, STOC`06).\nL16: >\nL17: > Our composition theorems show that ZK-IOPs can be modularly designed by composing sub-protocols, and ZK of the composed system follows seamlessly from the ZK guarantees of its building blocks.\nL18: Using our composition theorems, we easily derive both new and known results on ZK-IOPs in various settings, including ZK preservation under parallel/sequential composition, ZK of IOPs for sumcheck and codeswitching, ZK of IOPs for NP using arithmetization and sumcheck, and ZK preservation under IOP proof composition (reproving a result of Bootle, Chiesa and Liu, EC`22).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove · GitHub (https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove/releases)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 140\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】\nL23:       * 【12†Secret protectionStop leaks before they start】\nL24: \nL25:     * EXPLORE\nL26:       * 【13†Why GitHub】\nL27:       * 【14†Documentation†docs.github.com】\nL28:       * 【15†Blog†github.blog】\nL29:       * 【16†Changelog†github.blog】\nL30:       * 【17†Marketplace】\nL31: \nL32: 【18†View all features】\nL33: \nL34:   * Solutions\nL35:     * BY COMPANY SIZE\nL36:       * 【19†Enterprises】\nL37:       * 【20†Small and medium teams】\nL38:       * 【21†Startups】\nL39:       * 【22†Nonprofits】\nL40: \nL41:     * BY USE CASE\nL42:       * 【23†App Modernization】\nL43:       * 【24†DevSecOps】\nL44:       * 【25†DevOps】\nL45:       * 【26†CI/CD】\nL46:       * 【27†View all use cases】\nL47: \nL48:     * BY INDUSTRY\nL49:     