igned out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. 【61†Lagrange-Labs】\nL116:   2. 【62†deep-prove】\nL117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * 【62†Code】\nL120:   * 【63†Issues14 (14)】\nL121:   * 【64†Pull requests9 (9)】\nL122:   * 【65†Actions】\nL123:   * 【66†Security and quality】\nL124:   * 【67†Insights】\nL125: \nL126: More items\nL127: \nL128: # Releases: Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove\nL129: \nL130: 【68†Releases】 【69†Tags】\nL131: \nL132: [Input: Find a release] [Input]\nL133: \nL134: Releases · Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove\nL135: ## There aren’t any releases here\nL136: \nL137: You can create a release to package software, along with release notes and links to binary files, for other people to use. Learn more about releases in 【70†our docs†docs.github.com】.\nL138: \nL139: You can’t perform that action at this time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCMU CSD PhD Blog - Algorithms for the Sum-check Protocol (https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~csd-phd-blog/2026/sumcheck-proving/)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~csd-phd-blog/2026/sumcheck-proving/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 177\nL0:   * 【0†Home】\nL1:   * 【1†Areas】\nL2:   * 【2†Tags】\nL3:   * 【3†RSS】\nL4:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL5: \nL6: 【5†Image: CSD Logo】\nL7: \nL8: 【6†CSD PhD Blog】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【0†Home】\nL11:   * 【1†Areas】\nL12:   * 【2†Tags】\nL13:   * 【3†RSS】\nL14:   * 【4†CSD†csd.cmu.edu】\nL15: ## Contents\nL16: \nL17:   * 【7†How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations】\nL18:   * 【8†Sum-Check Protocol Overview】\nL19:   * 【9†Brief Interlude on Multilinear Polynomials】\nL20:     * 【10†Example: Arithmetizing a Batched Zero-Check】\nL21:   * 【11†Existing Algorithms for Sum-Check】\nL22:     * 【12†Linear-time algorithm】\nL23:     * 【13†Streaming algorithm with logarithmic space】\nL24:   * 【14†New Idea: Round Batching and its Benefits】\nL25:     * 【15†Why does round batching help?】\nL26:   * 【16†Conclusion】\nL27:   * 【17†Citations:】\nL28: # 【18†Algorithms for the Sum-check Protocol】\nL29: \nL30: 2026-09-01 | 【19†Quang Dao†quangvdao.github.io】\nL31: ## How to Verify Private (or Expensive) Computations\nL32: \nL33: Suppose a hospital runs a statistical analysis over millions of patient records to determine whether a drug is effective. The computation takes days of cluster time, and the raw data cannot be shared due to privacy regulations. A regulatory agency needs to trust the result—but cannot re-run the computation or inspect the data. How can they be confident the analysis was performed correctly?\nL34: This is an instance of a broader challenge: we increasingly rely on computational results we cannot feasibly re-check. Sometimes the data is private, so other parties cannot re-run the computation at all. Other times the data is public but the computation is simply too expensive to repeat.\nL35: Cryptographic zero-knowledge proof systems address this dilemma. They let an untrusted prover convince a verifier that some private data satisfies a public statement. For instance, you can prove that you are over 18 or that your account has sufficient balance without revealing your birthdate or financial history. Proofs are short and fast to verify—milliseconds rather than hours or days—so the savings compound when many parties need to check the same result.\nL36: Over the past decade, cryptographic proof systems have matured from theoretical curiosities into practical tools, with applications ranging from age verification [1] to blockchain scalability [2]. However, generating a proof is still orders of magnitude slower than running the computation natively, making prover efficiency a central challenge.\nL37: Many state-of-the-art proof systems owe much of their performance to a classical protocol from 1992: the sum-check protocol [4]. Sum-check’s very success, however, has made it a bottleneck: it often consumes the majority of the total proving time. In this blog post, I will introduce the sum-check protocol, detail well-known algorithms for its prover, and present a new technique that speeds up the prover in practical settings [9].\nL38: ## Sum-Check Protocol Overview\nL39: \nL40: The sum-check protocol [4] is an interactive proof that allows an untrusted prover to convince a computationally limited verifier of the value of a very large sum. Informally, the verifier wants to check a sum of many evaluations of a multivariate polynomial over a large product domain, but would like to avoid explicitly computing all of those values.\nL41: We will require two properties from the protocol: completeness (an honest prover always convinces the verifier) and soundness (a cheating prover is caught with overwhelming probability). After describing the protocol, we will verify that sum-check satisfies both.\nL42: Formally, we fix a finite field \\( \\mathbb{F} \\) (a number system with addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, but only finitely many elements) and a multivariate polynomial \\( p(X_1, \\dots, X_n) \\in \\mathbb{F}[X_1,\\dots, X_n] \\), of degree bounded by \\( d \\) in each variable. The sum-check claim is then $$ \\sum_{x_1 \\in H_1, \\dots, x_n \\in H_n} p(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = c, $$ for some evaluation domains \\( H_1, \\dots, H_n \\subseteq \\mathbb{F} \\) and a claimed value \\( c \\in \\mathbb{F} \\).\nL43: In most applications, and for the remainder of this blog post, we restrict the inputs to the Boolean hypercube , namely the domain \\( H_1 = \\dots = H_n = \\{0,1\\} \\). The claimed value \\( c \\) remains an arbitrary element of \\( \\mathbb{F} \\).\nL44: The verifier knows \\( p \\), or at least has query access to \\( p \\) (meaning it can ask for evaluations of \\( p \\) at chosen points), but wants to use this access as little as possible. Naively, the verifier could evaluate \\( p \\) on all \\( 2^n \\) points of \\( \\{0,1\\}^n \\) and sum the results, which takes work on the order of \\( O(2^n) \\).\nL45: The key idea of sum-check is that, by interacting with an untrusted prover who supplies additional “auxiliary” polynomials, the verifier can reduce the work of checking the original claim to checking a related claim about \\( p \\) at a single randomly chosen point.\nL46: In particular, the data that the prover sends at each round are the “one-dimensional” slices of this multivariate polynomial. In the first round, the prover sends the univariate polynomial $$ s_1(X) = \\sum_{(x_2,\\dots,x_n) \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n-1}} p(X, x_2,\\dots,x_n). $$ If the original claim is correct, then \\( s_1(X) \\) has degree at most \\( d \\), and moreover \\( s_1(0) + s_1(1) = c \\). The verifier checks precisely these two conditions, and rejects if either fails.\nL47: If both checks pass, the verifier samples a random challenge \\( r_1 \\gets \\mathbb{F} \\) and sends it to the prover. This random point forces consistency: if the prover sent an incorrect low-degree \\( s_1 \\), then with high probability (at least \\( 1 - d / \\lvert \\mathbb{F} \\rvert \\)) it disagrees with the honest slice at \\( X=r_1 \\). Over a sufficiently large field (e.g., at least 128-bit), this error probability is negligible. We discuss why this is the case under soundness below.\nL48: After this first round of interaction, the prover and verifier have effectively reduced the problem to showing that $$ \\sum_{(x_2,\\dots, x_n) \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n-1}} p_1(x_2, \\dots, x_n) = c_1, $$ where we define \\( p_1(x_2,\\dots,x_n) := p(r_1, x_2,\\dots,x_n) \\) and \\( c_1 := s_1(r_1) \\). That is, we have “bound” the first variable to \\( r_1 \\) (where “bound” here means “substituted in,” not lower or upper bound) and now reduce to a new sum-check instance in \\( n-1 \\) variables.\nL49: The protocol then repeats the same pattern on this new instance: in the second round, the prover sends a univariate polynomial $$ s_2(X) = \\sum_{(x_3,\\dots,x_n) \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n-2}} p_1(X, x_3,\\dots,x_n), $$ the verifier again checks the degree and the equation \\( s_2(0) + s_2(1) = c_1 \\), samples a fresh random \\( r_2 \\gets \\mathbb{F} \\), sets the new claim \\( c_2 := s_2(r_2) \\), and continues.\nL50: After \\( n \\) rounds, all variables have been fixed to random challenges \\( r_1,\\dots,r_n \\in \\mathbb{F} \\), and the verifier is left with a single claim of the form $$ p(r_1,\\dots,r_n) = c_n $$ that it can check directly using its query access to \\( p \\).\nL51: What properties does the sum-check protocol satisfy? The first is completeness . If the original sum claim is correct and the prover follows the rules, then every round’s check passes, and at the end we really do have \\( p(r_1,\\dots,r_n) = c_n \\), so the verifier accepts.\nL52: The more interesting property is soundness , which is about what happens when the original claim is false. In that case, no matter how a (possibly malicious) prover behaves, at some round it must send a polynomial that is not the “right” one.\nL53: This is exactly the situation we previewed when introducing the random challenge \\( r_1 \\): by the Schwartz–Zippel lemma [5], two different degree-\\( d \\) polynomials over a field agree on at most a \\( d / \\lvert \\mathbb{F}\\rvert \\) fraction of points, so a random challenge \\( r_i \\) catches the lie except with that probability. With a large field, this is negligible.\nL54: ## Brief Interlude on Multilinear Polynomials\nL55: So far, we have only described sum-check as a protocol on polynomials over finite fields.\nL56: But how does this relate to any computation performed in the real world? The key bridge between the two is called the arithmetization of the computation: we first transform the computation trace—such as the values of all registers over time, or the sequence of memory accesses and intermediate constraint values—into polynomials, and then we use sum-check to reason about those polynomials instead of the original computation.\nL57: In many sum-check-based proof systems, the traces and constraint tables are represented as multilinear polynomials . A multilinear polynomial is a multivariate polynomial where each variable appears with degree at most \\( 1 \\). For instance, $$ q(X_1, X_2, X_3) = 3X_1 X_3 + 2X_2 + 5 $$ is multilinear, while \\( X_1^2 + X_2 \\) is not. A key fact is that a multilinear polynomial in \\( n \\) variables is uniquely determined by its values on the \\( 2^n \\) points of the Boolean hypercube \\( \\{0,1\\}^n \\).\nL58: This gives a natural way to encode a length-\\( 2^n \\) vector as a multilinear polynomial: the polynomial’s values on the Boolean hypercube are exactly the entries of the vector.\nL59: We can also make this encoding precise with a mathematical formula. Given a function \\( p : \\{0,1\\}^n \\to \\mathbb{F} \\) (for example, a vector of trace or constraint values indexed by \\( y \\in \\{0,1\\}^n \\)), its multilinear extension is the unique multilinear polynomial \\( \\widetilde{p}(X_1,\\dots,X_n) \\) that agrees with \\( p \\) on all Boolean points.\nL60: The formula for the multilinear extension is as follows: $$ \\widetilde{p}(X_1,\\dots, X_n) = \\sum_{y \\in \\{0,1\\}^n} \\widetilde{eq}(\\vec{X}, y) \\cdot p(y), $$ where the equality polynomial \\( \\widetilde{eq} \\) is defined by $$ \\widetilde{eq}(\\vec{X}, \\vec{Y}) = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\big((1 - X_i)(1 - Y_i) + X_i Y_i\\big).\nL61: $$ You can think of \\( \\widetilde{eq} \\) as an indicator for equality on the Boolean hypercube: for any \\( \\vec{x}, \\vec{y} \\in \\{0,1\\}^n \\), \\( \\widetilde{eq}(\\vec{x}, \\vec{y}) = 1 \\) if \\( \\vec{x} = \\vec{y} \\) and \\( 0 \\) otherwise. Plugging this into the formula above, each term in the sum “picks out” \\( p(y) \\) at a single Boolean point.\nL62: As a concrete example, consider the vector \\( [3, 1, 4, 2] \\). We index its entries by the points of \\( \\{0,1\\}^2 \\): $$ p(0,0) = 3, \\quad p(0,1) = 1, \\quad p(1,0) = 4, \\quad p(1,1) = 2. $$ Using the formula above, the multilinear extension is $$ \\begin{aligned} \\widetilde{p}(X_1, X_2) &= 3 \\cdot (1 - X_1)(1 - X_2) + 1 \\cdot (1 - X_1)X_2 + 4 \\cdot X_1(1 - X_2) + 2 \\cdot X_1 X_2 \\\\ &= 3 - 2X_2 + X_1 - X_1 X_2.\nL63: \\end{aligned}$$ You can verify that \\( \\widetilde{p} \\) recovers the original vector on Boolean inputs. Moreover, the sum of the vector entries equals the sum of \\( \\widetilde{p} \\) over the hypercube: $$ \\sum_{(x_1, x_2) \\in \\{0,1\\}^2} \\widetilde{p}(x_1, x_2) = 3 + 1 + 4 + 2 = 10. $$ Verifying this sum is exactly a sum-check instance.\nL64: ### Example: Arithmetizing a Batched Zero-Check\nL65: \nL66: Now that we have seen multilinear polynomials, let’s see how they allow us to turn common constraints into sum-check instances.\nL67: \nL68: Consider a tiny 4-cycle execution trace of a program containing only two instructions, either add two numbers (think of them as field elements) or multiply two numbers:\nL69: Cycle \\( i \\)  | Instruction  | \\( x(i) \\)  | \\( y(i) \\)  | \\( z(i) \\)\nL70: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL71: 0  | ADD  | 3  | 5  | 8\nL72: 1  | MUL  | 2  | 6  | 12\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1704.02086] A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications (https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02086)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.02086\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 175\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computational Complexity\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Apr 2017]\nL9: # Title:A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Alessandro Chiesa】, 【6†Michael A. Forbes】, 【7†Nicholas Spooner】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications, by Alessandro Chiesa and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【8†View PDF】 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Many seminal results in Interactive Proofs (IPs) use algebraic techniques based on low-degree polynomials, the study of which is pervasive in theoretical computer science. Unfortunately, known methods for endowing such proofs with zero knowledge guarantees do not retain this rich algebraic structure.\nL17: > In this work, we develop algebraic techniques for obtaining zero knowledge variants of proof protocols in a way that leverages and preserves their algebraic structure. Our constructions achieve unconditional (perfect) zero knowledge in the Interactive Probabilistically Checkable Proof (IPCP) model of Kalai and Raz [KR08] (the prover first sends a PCP oracle, then the prover and verifier engage in an Interactive Proof in which the verifier may query the PCP).\nL18: > Our main result is a zero knowledge variant of the sumcheck protocol [LFKN92] in the IPCP model. The sumcheck protocol is a key building block in many IPs, including the protocol for polynomial-space computation due to Shamir [Sha92], and the protocol for parallel computation due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum [GKR15]. A core component of our result is an algebraic commitment scheme, whose hiding property is guaranteed by algebraic query complexity lower bounds [AW09,JKRS09].\nL19: This commitment scheme can then be used to considerably strengthen our previous work [BCFGRS16] that gives a sumcheck protocol with much weaker zero knowledge guarantees, itself using algebraic techniques based on algorithms for polynomial identity testing [RS05,BW04].\nL20: > We demonstrate the applicability of our techniques by deriving zero knowledge variants of well-known protocols based on algebraic techniques, including the protocols of Shamir and of Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum, as well as the protocol of Babai, Fortnow, and Lund [BFL91].\nL21: Subjects:  | Computational Complexity (cs.CC); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL22: Cite as:  | 【10†arXiv:1704.02086】 [cs.CC]\nL23:    | (or 【11†arXiv:1704.02086v1】 [cs.CC] for this version)\nL24:    | 【12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1704.02086†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Michael A. Forbes [【13†view email】]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 7 Apr 2017 03:54:42 UTC (484 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled A Zero Knowledge Sumcheck and its Applications, by Alessandro Chiesa and 2 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * 【8†View PDF】\nL37:   * 【9†HTML (experimental)】\nL38:   * 【14†TeX Source】\nL39: \nL40: 【15†view license】\nL41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * 【16†Audio Summary】\nL45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.CC\nL48: \nL49: 【17†< prev】   |   【18†next >】\nL50: \nL51: 【19†new】 | 【20†recent】 | 【21†2017-04】\nL52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: 【22†cs】\nL56: 【23†cs.CR】\nL57: \nL58: ### References & Citations\nL59: \nL60:   * 【24†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL63: ### 【27†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de】 - CS Bibliography\nL64: \nL65: 【28†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de】 | 【29†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL66: \nL67: 【30†Alessandro Chiesa†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL68: 【31†Michael A. Forbes†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL69: 【32†Nicholas Spooner†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL70: \nL71: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL72: \nL73: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL74: \nL75: [Button: ×]\nL76: \nL77: loading...\nL78: \nL79: Data provided by:\nL80: \nL81: ### Bookmark\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL84: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL87: \nL88: Bibliographic Explorer (【33†What is the Explorer?†info.arxi